A common issue I find is when using a `when` in a composable and not propagating the `modifier` parameter to all branches, typically when a new branch is added. For example:
```kotlin
@Composable
fun SomeScreenContent(
  state: SomeViewState,
  modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
) {
  when (state) {
    is SomeViewState.Loading -> SomeScreenLoadingContent()
    is SomeViewState.Loaded -> SomeScreenLoadedContent(modifier)
  }
}
```
I'd like to see a warning/error on the `is SomeViewState.Loading -> SomeScreenLoadingContent()` branch saying that you probably want to pass the `modifier` parameter. The exception is if the composable doesn't accept a modifier.

Version: 1.6.1

`isTypeMutable` falls back to comparing the resolved class’s simple name against `KnownMutableCommonTypesSimpleNames`:

`return uParamClass.javaPsi.name in KnownMutableCommonTypesSimpleNames`

So any class whose simple name is on that list gets flagged whatever its package. UAST also counts an extension receiver as a parameter so receiver types are checked too.

Repro:

```kotlin
@Stable
object Banner {
    @Stable
    object Stack
}

@Composable
fun Banner.Stack.Default(text: String) { /* ... */ }
// ComposeMutableParameters: reported on `Banner.Stack`
```

`Banner.Stack` is an immutable object; it’s flagged only because "Stack" (meant for `java.util.Stack`) is on the list.

Expected: no report. Possible fixes:

* Match on fully qualified names (java.util.Stack, java.util.Vector, …) instead of simple names.
* Skip extension/context receivers.
